7592186403064 1069 "q-54242" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403064 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=-x+3.$" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403064 1072 #uuid "6de12d86-c297-540f-8fcc-949f18bd056f" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403064 1074 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-x+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ does not intersect the line $y=-x+3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85c9bea054f2e246d62864b022e53a6f1d23886b0f0c9f8c80c8e0a5b1be9cd8.png)" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403066 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403067 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403068 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403069 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403070 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1077 "selection" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1079 #edb/ref 17592186403068 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403065 1080 #uuid "ea675cb1-71da-58ac-9964-d