 #uuid "3d511e4f-8803-5021-b5c2-53a7b4de5acf" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403130 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403132 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403133 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403134 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403135 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403136 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1077 "selection" 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1079 #edb/ref 17592186403135 13194139533491 17592186045585 true] [17592186403131 1080 #uuid "090c78d3-3a0c-5fa4-b1fc-517e968e51e7" 13194139533